    "case_id": "759",
    "case_summary": "A 29-year-old Chinese male patient with no underlying disease was hospitalized twice for injuries from a car accident. Initial admission was for open abdominal injury, followed by emergency surgery and discharge after 5 days. Two days later, he was re-admitted due to persistent lower abdominal pain. During the second hospitalization, he underwent emergency surgery for intestinal issues. After surgery, his condition deteriorated with high fever.\n\nOn second admission: Temperature 36.8°C, blood pressure 135/67 mmHg. Physical exam showed total abdominal tenderness, especially in lower abdomen, with rebound pain and muscle tension. Post-surgery: Temperature rose to 39.3°C.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Initial tests on second admission showed WBC 16.62 x109/L, neutrophils 87.80%.Post-surgery: WBC 22.68 x109/L, neutrophils 94.1%- Imaging studies:CT scan showed intestinal perforation, peritonitis, high density shadow in ascending colon, and thickening/swelling of ascending colon and ileum segments.- Microbiology:Blood culture: Positive in bilateral anaerobic bottles (positive time 65.5h and 78.5h)Gram stain: Gram-negative bacteriaCulture: Slow-growing, moist, gray-white colonies after 3 daysMALDI-TOF MS score: 2.11016S rRNA sequencing: 100% match to reference strainImage Title: Colony on blood agar plateDescription: Moist, gray-white bacterial colonies after 3 days of anaerobic culture",
